/* ==========================================================================
   SCREEN

   The full-screen layer a document opens into: how it covers the game, how it
   fades, and the two stacking helpers every panel is built from. The overlay
   is the same idea one level up - a screen standing over a screen.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ==========================================================================
   SCREEN
   ========================================================================== */

.screen {
    -unity-font-definition:
        url("project://database/Assets/Collaborators/Qewzdl/Fonts/Vectipede%20Th%20SDF.asset?fileID=11400000&guid=f62a91bdc6ed1864f8d536b0db2daaff&type=2#Vectipede Th SDF");

    position: absolute;

    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;

    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;

    background-color: var(--veil);

    opacity: 0;

    /* Fading, not moving: an overshoot on opacity has nowhere to overshoot to
       and is clipped at one, which only makes the screen arrive early. The
       overshoot belongs to the things that travel. */
    transition-property: opacity;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-screen);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease-standard);
}


.screen--open {
    opacity: 1;
}


/* A screen that stands beside what is behind it rather than over it: the menu
   goes to one side and the rest of the frame is left to the scene. Anything
   the camera is looking at becomes the backdrop. */

.screen--left {
    align-items: flex-start;

    background-color: var(--transparent);
}


/* ==========================================================================
   MASTHEAD

   The name of the game, lifted out of the column and set on the screen.
   Placed rather than stacked: it holds the corner while the column runs the
   height of the frame past it, and the two stop reading as one box with a
   heading on top.

   Its left edge lines up with the column's text, so the arrangement looks
   composed rather than merely uncentred - and its right end runs off the column
   onto the picture, which is why the name is set here and not inside the panel.

   It has to be written after the panel in the markup. Nothing in USS says so:
   both are absolutely placed, neither has a z-index, and the one declared later
   is the one on top. While the column was only as tall as its contents the name
   sat in clear air above it and the order never came up; the moment the column
   ran the full height it covered the name with nine tenths of near-black, and a
   masthead at any colour at all came out at a twentieth of it.
   ========================================================================== */

.masthead {
    position: absolute;

    left: var(--space-xl);
    top: var(--space-xl);
}


.masthead .title {
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   BACKDROP

   What the screen stands in front of. Sits behind everything else in the
   document and takes no clicks, so the menu above it behaves as though it
   were not there.

   The picture is set here and nowhere else - one line to change the whole
   frame - and it is cropped rather than squashed, because a menu is seen at
   whatever shape the player's window happens to be.
   ========================================================================== */

.backdrop {
    position: absolute;

    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;

    background-color: var(--backdrop);

    -unity-background-scale-mode: scale-and-crop;
}


/* Laid over the picture rather than mixed into it: art is chosen for how it
   looks, not for how much text it can carry, and this is the dial that makes
   any of it quiet enough to read across. */

.backdrop__shade {
    flex-grow: 1;

    background-color: var(--backdrop-shade);
}


/* ==========================================================================
   MENU

   The main menu is not a panel, and this is where it stopped being one.

   It wore the lobby's column for a long time, which is how a title screen ended
   up with a 460-wide wall painted over the picture it was built around. Reading
   back, the wall was never its idea: --veil went to nine tenths because the
   lobby stands over a lit room and a tenth of that is a bright shape moving
   behind the text. The lobby has since been given a wall of its own. The main
   menu kept the opacity, and the reason for it had never applied here.

   The two are different objects. The lobby is a document - a roster somebody
   sits and watches, settings, four actions - and it needs a ground to be read
   against. The main menu is four words and a name field over a photograph, and
   the photograph is the screen. It needs no ground: the shade already laid over
   the picture is what makes it readable, and that is the dial if it ever is not.

   So it is placed rather than contained, the way the masthead already is. The
   name holds the top corner, the menu holds the bottom one, and what is between
   them is the picture. They share a left edge, which is what makes the two read
   as one composition rather than as two things that happen to be on the left.
   ========================================================================== */

.menu {
    position: absolute;

    left: var(--space-xl);
    bottom: var(--space-xl);

    /* What the column's text column measured: the rail's 460 with its padding
       taken off both sides. Nothing here needs a width except the name field,
       and the name field is the width it has always been. */
    width: 364px;
}


/* Left, like everything else down this edge. The base hint is centred because
   most of them sit in a dialog. */

.menu .hint {
    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ENTRIES

   A word rather than a box. Four framed rectangles stacked up read as a form to
   be filled in; these are places to go, and what is left when the frame comes
   off is the word, lighting the way words light everywhere else.

   Written here rather than shared with the lobby's column. The two want the
   same thing today and they are two screens, so they get two rules - that is
   the whole point of pulling them apart, and a shared selector would have put
   them back together the first time either one needed to change.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.menu .stack .button {
    border-width: 0;

    padding-top: var(--space-m);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-m);

    /* No indent. The word lines up with the name field above it and with the
       masthead at the far corner, which is the whole reason the box came off. */
    padding-left: 0;
    padding-right: 0;

    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}


/* The word lights; it does not fill. A button in a panel inverts under the
   pointer because it has an edge for the colour to come from. An entry here has
   none, and a fill would be a slab arriving out of nothing over the picture. */

.menu .stack .button:hover:enabled {
    color: var(--accent);

    background-color: var(--transparent);
}


.menu .stack .button:active:enabled {
    color: var(--accent-pressed);

    background-color: var(--transparent);
}


/* Rests a step down from the accent so the pointer has somewhere to take it.
   Brighter than the three words beside it, and not already at the top. */

.menu .stack .button--confirm {
    color: var(--text);
}


/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ARRIVAL

   A screen assembles itself rather than appearing whole: the parts follow one
   after another, which reads as a place being opened instead of a screenshot.

   Said of a screen rather than of a rail. It was .rail__item, on the theory
   that a column was the only thing that arrived this way, and the main menu has
   no rail any more - its masthead and its entries stagger in exactly the same
   way while sitting on a picture.

   USS cannot count children, so the order is written into the markup. Ugly
   there, honest here: the alternative is a script that exists only to stagger
   four labels.

   The buttons arrive as one block rather than one by one. A delay in USS
   applies to everything the element transitions, so a stagger class on a
   button would also delay the colour it takes when the pointer reaches it -
   and a menu item that lights up a fifth of a second late feels broken in a
   way no entrance is worth.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.enter {
    opacity: 0;

    translate: 0 12px;

    transition-property:
        opacity,
        translate;

    transition-duration: var(--motion-screen);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease-standard);
}


.screen--open .enter {
    opacity: 1;

    translate: 0 0;
}


.enter--2 {
    transition-delay: 0.04s;
}


.enter--3 {
    transition-delay: 0.08s;
}


.enter--4 {
    transition-delay: 0.12s;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   LAYOUT
   ========================================================================== */

.stack {
    flex-grow: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
}


/* The gap a column of buttons keeps between its entries, the same way
   .footer .button is the gap a row keeps between its own. Here it is none.

   A button carries --space-xs above and below itself, and in a column those
   met and held four menu entries apart as four separate things. They are one
   list, so they are stacked: the entries touch, and what runs between any two
   of them is their two edges meeting rather than a gap. Nothing above the
   first or below the last either - the ends belong to whatever the column
   sits in, the panel's padding in the menu and the rule over the actions in
   the lobby. */

.stack .button {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
}


.row {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   OVERLAY
   ========================================================================== */

/* A screen standing over a screen, and it fades like one. Everything else in
   the interface arrives: the layer fades, the column slides, the panel rises.
   A dialog that cut straight in was the one moment the whole thing looked
   like a menu instead of a place.

   Faster than a screen, though. This one is answering a question the player
   just asked, and a wait they caused themselves is the one they notice. */

.overlay {
    position: absolute;

    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;

    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;

    background-color: var(--veil);

    opacity: 0;

    transition-property: opacity;
    transition-duration: var(--motion-normal);
    transition-timing-function: var(--ease-standard);
}


.overlay--open {
    opacity: 1;
}


